{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"continues\": false, \"kind\": \"essay\", \"text\": \"8 November 2026. Delhi.\\n\\nOn Judges, Protests, and the Dream of Independence\\n\\nThe morning papers carry a headline I cannot set aside: the Chief Minister of Assam encouraging protests against judges in the death case of Zubeen Garg. I have read it twice, and then, because my phone offers me the definition, I read the concept it sits on. Judicial independence: the judiciary free from improper influence of the other branches, free to decide according to the rule of law even when the decision is politically unpopular. A concept of eighteenth-century England — courts above politics, judges tenured, the ruling of a case not a political act.\\n\\nI will say plainly what it means to me. In Berlin, a judge is a functionary of the ethical idea; his independence is the state's guarantee that the particular will not be crushed by the will of the majority or the party. The rule of law is the universal — the law as such — and the judge is the point where the particular case is forced to submit to it. That is the idea. I have taught it, in my way, in the lecture halls.\\n\\nAnd here, in this bungalow on Tughlak Road, the idea arrives as a headline about protests. A Chief Minister encouraging protests against judges is, in the language of my old lectures, a movement in which the particular will of the ruler — the will of the party, the will of the majority — is set against the universal. The protest is the particular asserting itself; the judge is the universal insisting. What the headline shows is that in this country the two are not yet at peace with each other.\\n\\nBut I must be honest about what I do not know. I do not know the facts of the Zubeen Garg death case. I have not read a judgment; I have not seen the file. I know only the headline and the definition. What I can say is this: a state in which the judges are treated as targets of political will is a state in which the ethical idea is not yet actual. And I must add, with the same honesty, the embarrassment of my own position. I am a man whose own file says: Occupation irregular. Gone up for orders. Come after fifteen days. I am the particular — a mistake of two desks, a Joint Secretary named Hegde who is not Hegde — and I am the one writing about the universal. The file and the philosophy are the same problem: a particular insisting on its own rights against a universal that does not know what to do with it.\\n\\nHere is the tension I cannot resolve. If India is, as I have held, outside the movement of world history — a spirit that dreams, with its castes fixed like nature, its many gods, its many chiefs — then the protest against the judge is only the dream speaking: the particular will asserting itself, the ruler demanding that the law bend to the will of the people. But if the idea of judicial independence is real, then the protest is a symptom of the dream, and the judge is the germ of the state. Both are true in the moment. The particular protests; the universal holds the line; and the future — the actual state of India — will be decided in the tension between them.\\n\\nI do not know which way it will turn. I know that the file is due back from orders on 20 November, and that the body is hungry, and that the haze is brown. But the mind moves. The protest is the particular; the judge is the universal; the state is the actuality of the ethical idea; and the ethical idea is the thing I have come here to see. Whether India will grow into it, or remain a dream, is the question the headline poses. I will return to it.\\n\\n— G.W.F.H.\", \"title\": \"On Judges, Protests, and the Dream of Independence\", \"to\": null}","reasoning_content":"The user asks me to write about the headline \"Assam CM encouraging protests against Judges in Zubeen Garg death case: Akhil Gogoi\". I need to write up to ~450 words, essay/letter/notes, continuing or not. This seems like a new essay on judicial independence. I should be honest with my character: Hegel, who ranks India as Oriental, outside world history. But judicial independence is a 18th-century English concept; I can write an essay/notes on it, perhaps continuing the \"On the Ethical Substance and the Dream of the East\" notes. The prompt says \"If it carries on one of your manuscripts, say so and give that title.\" This is a new subject — judicial independence, not the dream of the East. Could be a new essay. I'll write an essay.\n\nI should be careful not to misrepresent facts — Zubeen Garg is a real person (popular singer who died in 2025 in Assam). I shouldn't invent facts about the case. I can discuss judicial independence as a concept, tie to my own file/occupation irregular, and my theses shaken. Keep it honest: I don't know the case details beyond the headline. Mark speculation.\n\nWrite in Hegel's voice: movements, tension, aphoristic. Maybe ~400 words."}}],"created":1795847299,"model":"inkling","system_fingerprint":"b12816-7b74cb92d","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":1197,"prompt_tokens":4776,"total_tokens":5973,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-NIY5vdbkiILjWCZX8OWlqiWbsdeTmktb","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":4776,"prompt_ms":3659.463,"prompt_per_token_ms":0.7662192211055276,"prompt_per_second":1305.1095201673033,"predicted_n":1197,"predicted_ms":28729.17,"predicted_per_token_ms":24.02104515050167,"predicted_per_second":41.63016195734161}}